{"reference":"Jeremiah 31","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":31,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The New Covenant","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Verses 31–34 name a “new covenant,” a renewed relationship promised to both the houses of Israel and Judah. Its features are God’s instruction, or Torah, written in people’s inward parts and hearts; shared knowledge of YHWH from least to greatest; and forgiveness. Within a chapter filled with return, rebuilding, planting, mourning, and comfort, these promises present restoration without pretending that the earlier loss has not occurred.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 31:1-40","Jeremiah 31:31-37"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_031_12d72f59380aa9fa59f5e53e"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Jeremiah in Its Larger Setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Jeremiah 30–33 forms a restoration collection within the received book of Jeremiah. The book as a whole brings together varied forms, including poetry, prose sermons, narratives, letters, headings, repeated accounts, oracles concerning nations, and an appendix; its long compositional and textual history remains a matter of scholarly discussion. This broader setting helps locate Jeremiah 31’s restoration language within a larger collection rather than treating it as an isolated passage.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_031_5f5c45432d04d38101175b55"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}